::JOUR_U05_L04_Q_Q01::[MLA.JOUR.PUB.01] Source: Collaborative Online Feature Package. Publication Task: A student newsroom team is preparing an online feature package about the school garden reopening. Draft Elements: The reporter wrote a 450-word feature. The photo editor added three images with captions. The copy editor left comments asking for a clearer transition between the garden history and the reopening date. The web editor needs a headline, deck, image order, alt text, and export-ready layout. Audience: Students, families, and community volunteers who may want to visit or help maintain the garden. Feedback Notes: One paragraph repeats the same background detail twice. One caption says students working but does not identify the garden bed. The final paragraph needs a clearer call to action for volunteers. Publication Requirement: The team must revise for clarity, respond to comments, maintain format-specific quality, and export a polished online article for the intended audience. Question 1: Which task should happen before export? {
~A. The team should export first and respond to comments later.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the visual accuracy, revision, evidence, audience, or publication requirement. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misleads the audience, ignores source evidence, skips revision, weakens format quality, or does not fit the publication purpose.
~B. The team should delete all comments without reading them.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the visual accuracy, revision, evidence, audience, or publication requirement. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misleads the audience, ignores source evidence, skips revision, weakens format quality, or does not fit the publication purpose.
=C. The team should resolve comments, revise for clarity, check captions and alt text, and then export.#Correct. Step 1: identify the production or publication skill in the standard. Step 2: connect the answer to the embedded journalism scenario. This answer works because it protects accuracy, uses evidence, improves clarity, follows format expectations, or supports the intended audience.
~D. The team should publish the draft before checking audience needs.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the visual accuracy, revision, evidence, audience, or publication requirement. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misleads the audience, ignores source evidence, skips revision, weakens format quality, or does not fit the publication purpose.
}
::JOUR_U05_L04_Q_Q02::[MLA.JOUR.NEWS.04] Source: Collaborative Online Feature Package. Publication Task: A student newsroom team is preparing an online feature package about the school garden reopening. Draft Elements: The reporter wrote a 450-word feature. The photo editor added three images with captions. The copy editor left comments asking for a clearer transition between the garden history and the reopening date. The web editor needs a headline, deck, image order, alt text, and export-ready layout. Audience: Students, families, and community volunteers who may want to visit or help maintain the garden. Feedback Notes: One paragraph repeats the same background detail twice. One caption says students working but does not identify the garden bed. The final paragraph needs a clearer call to action for volunteers. Publication Requirement: The team must revise for clarity, respond to comments, maintain format-specific quality, and export a polished online article for the intended audience. Question 2: Which revision best addresses repeated background? {
=A. The repeated background should be combined or removed so the article flows clearly into the reopening date.#Correct. Step 1: identify the production or publication skill in the standard. Step 2: connect the answer to the embedded journalism scenario. This answer works because it protects accuracy, uses evidence, improves clarity, follows format expectations, or supports the intended audience.
~B. The repeated background should stay because repetition always helps readers.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the visual accuracy, revision, evidence, audience, or publication requirement. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misleads the audience, ignores source evidence, skips revision, weakens format quality, or does not fit the publication purpose.
~C. The repeated background should be moved into every section.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the visual accuracy, revision, evidence, audience, or publication requirement. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misleads the audience, ignores source evidence, skips revision, weakens format quality, or does not fit the publication purpose.
~D. The repeated background should be replaced with unrelated garden facts.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the visual accuracy, revision, evidence, audience, or publication requirement. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misleads the audience, ignores source evidence, skips revision, weakens format quality, or does not fit the publication purpose.
}
::JOUR_U05_L04_Q_Q03::[MLA.JOUR.ETH.01] Source: Collaborative Online Feature Package. Publication Task: A student newsroom team is preparing an online feature package about the school garden reopening. Draft Elements: The reporter wrote a 450-word feature. The photo editor added three images with captions. The copy editor left comments asking for a clearer transition between the garden history and the reopening date. The web editor needs a headline, deck, image order, alt text, and export-ready layout. Audience: Students, families, and community volunteers who may want to visit or help maintain the garden. Feedback Notes: One paragraph repeats the same background detail twice. One caption says students working but does not identify the garden bed. The final paragraph needs a clearer call to action for volunteers. Publication Requirement: The team must revise for clarity, respond to comments, maintain format-specific quality, and export a polished online article for the intended audience. Question 3: How should the weak caption be improved? {
~A. The caption is fine because students working is specific enough.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the visual accuracy, revision, evidence, audience, or publication requirement. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misleads the audience, ignores source evidence, skips revision, weakens format quality, or does not fit the publication purpose.
~B. The caption should include a joke instead of identifying the image.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the visual accuracy, revision, evidence, audience, or publication requirement. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misleads the audience, ignores source evidence, skips revision, weakens format quality, or does not fit the publication purpose.
~C. The caption should be removed from all images.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the visual accuracy, revision, evidence, audience, or publication requirement. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misleads the audience, ignores source evidence, skips revision, weakens format quality, or does not fit the publication purpose.
=D. The caption should identify the garden bed and explain what students are doing.#Correct. Step 1: identify the production or publication skill in the standard. Step 2: connect the answer to the embedded journalism scenario. This answer works because it protects accuracy, uses evidence, improves clarity, follows format expectations, or supports the intended audience.
}
::JOUR_U05_L04_Q_Q04::[MLA.JOUR.PUB.01] Source: Collaborative Online Feature Package. Publication Task: A student newsroom team is preparing an online feature package about the school garden reopening. Draft Elements: The reporter wrote a 450-word feature. The photo editor added three images with captions. The copy editor left comments asking for a clearer transition between the garden history and the reopening date. The web editor needs a headline, deck, image order, alt text, and export-ready layout. Audience: Students, families, and community volunteers who may want to visit or help maintain the garden. Feedback Notes: One paragraph repeats the same background detail twice. One caption says students working but does not identify the garden bed. The final paragraph needs a clearer call to action for volunteers. Publication Requirement: The team must revise for clarity, respond to comments, maintain format-specific quality, and export a polished online article for the intended audience. Question 4: Why is alt text needed? {
~A. Alt text is unnecessary when photos are colorful.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the visual accuracy, revision, evidence, audience, or publication requirement. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misleads the audience, ignores source evidence, skips revision, weakens format quality, or does not fit the publication purpose.
=B. Alt text helps online readers who use screen readers understand the images.#Correct. Step 1: identify the production or publication skill in the standard. Step 2: connect the answer to the embedded journalism scenario. This answer works because it protects accuracy, uses evidence, improves clarity, follows format expectations, or supports the intended audience.
~C. Alt text should be added only after readers complain.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the visual accuracy, revision, evidence, audience, or publication requirement. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misleads the audience, ignores source evidence, skips revision, weakens format quality, or does not fit the publication purpose.
~D. Alt text should describe the reporter opinion instead of the image.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the visual accuracy, revision, evidence, audience, or publication requirement. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misleads the audience, ignores source evidence, skips revision, weakens format quality, or does not fit the publication purpose.
}
::JOUR_U05_L04_Q_Q05::[MLA.JOUR.NEWS.04] Source: Collaborative Online Feature Package. Publication Task: A student newsroom team is preparing an online feature package about the school garden reopening. Draft Elements: The reporter wrote a 450-word feature. The photo editor added three images with captions. The copy editor left comments asking for a clearer transition between the garden history and the reopening date. The web editor needs a headline, deck, image order, alt text, and export-ready layout. Audience: Students, families, and community volunteers who may want to visit or help maintain the garden. Feedback Notes: One paragraph repeats the same background detail twice. One caption says students working but does not identify the garden bed. The final paragraph needs a clearer call to action for volunteers. Publication Requirement: The team must revise for clarity, respond to comments, maintain format-specific quality, and export a polished online article for the intended audience. Question 5: Which choice best describes publication-ready work? {
~A. Publication-ready work only needs pictures.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the visual accuracy, revision, evidence, audience, or publication requirement. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misleads the audience, ignores source evidence, skips revision, weakens format quality, or does not fit the publication purpose.
~B. Publication-ready work means the article is long enough.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the visual accuracy, revision, evidence, audience, or publication requirement. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misleads the audience, ignores source evidence, skips revision, weakens format quality, or does not fit the publication purpose.
=C. Publication-ready work is revised, audience-focused, formatted correctly, accessible, and ready to share.#Correct. Step 1: identify the production or publication skill in the standard. Step 2: connect the answer to the embedded journalism scenario. This answer works because it protects accuracy, uses evidence, improves clarity, follows format expectations, or supports the intended audience.
~D. Publication-ready work means the writer likes the first draft.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the visual accuracy, revision, evidence, audience, or publication requirement. Step 2: compare this option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it misleads the audience, ignores source evidence, skips revision, weakens format quality, or does not fit the publication purpose.
}
